+
+#### Pruning finished branches and worktrees
+
+Merged branches and their worktrees are pruned for you, so they do not pile up. Cleanup asks the pull request, not `git branch -d` or `git cherry`, because a squash merge rewrites history and makes both call a finished branch unmerged.
+
+```bash
+ta cleanup --only branches --dry-run   # Show each branch, the evidence and what would happen
+ta cleanup --only branches             # Delete the safe ones, ask about the rest
+```
+
+A local branch is deleted without asking only when one of these holds:
+
+- its pull request is merged and the branch has no commits beyond the PR head, or
+- its tip is already contained in the default branch.
+
+Everything else is kept:
+
+| Situation | What happens |
+|-----------|--------------|
+| Pull request still open | Kept, with the reason shown |
+| No pull request, or one closed without merging | A question (the branch may hold unmerged work) |
+| Commits beyond the merged PR's head | A question, with the count |
+| Checked out in a worktree with uncommitted or untracked files | A question that lists the files; nothing is removed |
+| The current branch or the default branch | Never touched |
+
+When a branch is deleted, its clean worktree is removed first, and the line `deleted branch <name> (tip <sha>); restore with git branch <name> <sha>` goes to `.ta/audit.jsonl` and `.ta/cleanup/branches.jsonl`. To bring one back, run the `git branch` command from that line. If the branch moved between the check and the delete, it is kept.
+
+Remote branches are never deleted (let GitHub's "Automatically delete head branches" do that). Cleanup only reports a remote branch whose PR was closed unmerged, with the `git push <remote> --delete <branch>` command if you want it gone.
+
+This runs by itself: right after `ta draft watch` sees your PR merge, and in the daemon's periodic cleanup even when `auto = "off"`. To turn it off, set `prune_branches = false` under `[cleanup]`.
